 # Primitive Representation
 
-Also called: Gaussian Primitives, Splatting Kernels
+Also called: Gaussian Primitives, Splat Primitives
 
 ## Overview
-Primitive Representation defines the geometric and parametric entities used to approximate a 3D scene in differentiable splatting methods, serving as the fundamental building blocks for volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. It solves the limitations of volumetric Gaussian mixtures—such as ambiguous geometry, poor mesh extraction, and inefficient representation of sharp edges—by defining explicit density functions and appearance attributes for each primitive [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods by varying the fundamental density function, dimensionality, or geometric support (e.g., from 3D ellipsoids to 2D surfels or Bézier surfaces) while maintaining the core splatting pipeline of projection and alpha compositing.
+Primitive Representation defines the geometric and appearance attributes of the explicit 3D primitives used to represent a scene in differentiable splatting methods. It solves the ambiguity and inefficiency of volumetric Gaussian mixtures for sharp geometry by defining the fundamental density function, shape, and color storage of each splat [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods by varying the fundamental density function (e.g., half-Gaussians, surfels) or the parameterization of appearance (e.g., Bézier triangles) while maintaining the core splatting pipeline of projection and alpha compositing.
 
 ## Shared principles
-All child methods share the core requirement of defining a 3D primitive with position, orientation, scale, and appearance attributes [G]. The standard 3D Gaussian primitive is defined by a mean $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ for position, a covariance $\Sigma$ for shape and orientation, and appearance attributes like Spherical Harmonics coefficients and opacity $\alpha$ [G]. Parameterized as $\Sigma^w = R S S^T R^T$ in world space, where $R$ is rotation and $S$ is scaling [G]. The rendering pipeline projects these 3D primitives to 2D screen space [G]. The covariance transforms via the view matrix $W$ and projection Jacobian $J$: $\Sigma = J W \Sigma^w W^T J^T$ [G]. The integral of the 3D density along the view direction yields the 2D splat density [G].
+All child methods share the core requirement of defining a 3D primitive with a position, orientation, scale, and appearance attributes [G]. The standard 3D Gaussian primitive is defined by a mean $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ for position, a covariance $\Sigma$ for shape and orientation, and appearance attributes like Spherical Harmonics coefficients and opacity $\alpha$ [G]. Parameterized as $\Sigma^w = R S S^T R^T$ in world space, where $R$ is rotation and $S$ is scaling [G]. The rendering pipeline projects these 3D primitives to 2D screen space [G]. The covariance transforms via the view matrix $W$ and projection Jacobian $J$: $\Sigma = J W \Sigma^w W^T J^T$ [G]. The integral of the 3D density along the view direction yields the 2D splat density [G].
 
